WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:10.170
In earlier episodes, we've seen plenty of
video footage about hair simulations and rendering.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:15.440
And today we're going to look at a cool new
technique that produces self-shadowing effects

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.340
for hair and fur.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.480
In this image pair, you can see this drastic
difference that shows how prominent this effect

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.070
is in the visual appearance of hair.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.330
Just look at that.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.330
Beautiful.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.240
But computing such a thing is extremely costly.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.480
Since we have a dense piece of geometry, for
instance, hundreds of thousands of hair strands,

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.800
we have to know how each one occludes the
other ones.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.160
This would take hopelessly long to compute.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.660
To even get a program that executes in a reasonable
amount of time, we clearly need to simplify

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.810
the problem further.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:53.800
An earlier technique takes a few planes that
cut the hair volume into layers.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.090
These planes are typically regularly spaced
outward from the light sources and it is much

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:03.969
easier to work with a handful of these volume
segments than with the full geometry.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:08.600
The more planes we use, the more layers we
obtain, and the higher quality results we

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.600
can expect.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.240
However, even if we can do this in real time,
we will produce unrealistic images when using

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.130
around 16 layers.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.310
Well of course, we should then crank up the
number of layers some more!

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:28.280
If we do that, for instance by now using 128
layers, we can expect better quality results,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.530
but we'll be able to process an image only
twice a second, which is far from competitive.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.740
And even then, the final results still contain
layering artifacts and are not very close

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.040
to the ground truth.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.820
There has to be a better way to do this.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:48.970
And with this new technique called Deep Opacity
Maps, these layers are chosen more wisely,

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:54.320
and this way, we can achieve higher quality
results with only using 3 layers, and it runs

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.259
easily in real time.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:59.600
It is also more memory efficient than previous
techniques.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.010
The key idea is that if we look at the hair
from the light source's point of view, we

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:10.129
can record how far away different parts of
the geometry are from the light source.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:14.989
Then, we can create the new layers further
and further away according to this shape.

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:20.090
This way, the layers are not planar anymore,
they adapt to the scene that we have at hand

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.620
and contain significantly more useful occlusion
information.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:31.019
As you can see, this new technique blows all
previous methods away and is incredibly simple.

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:37.849
I have found an implementation from Philip
Rideout, the link to this is available in

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:39.109
the video description.

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:43.219
If you have found more, let me know and I'll
include your findings in the video description

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:45.269
for the fellow tinkerers out there.

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:49.309
The paper is ample in comparisons, make sure
to have a look at that too.

00:02:49.309 --> 00:02:55.379
And sometimes I get some messages saying "Károly,
why do you bother covering papers from so

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:58.309
many years ago, it doesn't make any sense!".

00:02:58.309 --> 00:03:03.719
And here you can see that part of the excitement
of Two Minute Papers is that the next episode

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:06.219
can be about absolutely anything.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:11.070
The series has been mostly focusing on computer
graphics and machine learning papers, but

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:15.669
don't forget, that we also have an episode
on whether we're living in a simulation, or

00:03:15.669 --> 00:03:18.499
the Dunning-Kruger effect and so much more.

00:03:18.499 --> 00:03:22.089
I've put a link to both of them in the video
description for your enjoyment.

00:03:22.089 --> 00:03:26.959
The other reason for covering older papers
is that a lot of people don't know about them

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:32.839
and if we can help just a tiny bit to make
sure these incredible works see more widespread

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.880
adoption, we've done our job well.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.080
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

